# NotebookLM → Initial Confluence API Documentation Workflow

When the user provides a notebook name or business use case name (e.g., `notebooklm Drupal` or `notebooklm "HCP Lead Creation"`), execute the full workflow below automatically without asking for extra input unless a notebook cannot be unambiguously identified.

This workflow runs **before code exists**. It generates initial Confluence API documentation pre-populated from JIRA stories, design notes, and architecture decisions captured in the notebook. Fields requiring actual code (GitHub URL, property files, DWL mappings) are left as `TBC`.

The output format follows the canonical section structure of `agents/documentation/rules/gen-docs-implementation.mdc`.

---

## Step 1 — Identify the Target Notebook

Call `notebook_list` (NotebookLM MCP) to retrieve all available notebooks.

Match the correct notebook using this priority order:
1. Exact or partial match of notebook title against the business use case name provided by the user
2. Partial match of notebook title against the JIRA story ID (e.g., `NGASIM-3336`)
3. If only one notebook exists, use it directly

If two or more notebooks remain as candidates after matching, present a numbered short list to the user and ask them to confirm with a single reply — do not proceed until confirmed.

---

## Step 2 — Get Notebook Details & Studio Notes (run in parallel)

Issue all three calls simultaneously once the notebook ID is known:

**2a. `notebook_get(notebook_id)`** — returns notebook metadata and the full list of sources with their IDs. Record every `source_id`.

**2b. `note_list(notebook_id)`** — fetches all Studio Notes from the notebook's Studio panel. These are user-authored notes such as:
- Sequence diagrams (mermaid syntax)
- Architecture sketches
- Decision logs
- Integration flow descriptions

Capture every note's title and full body content verbatim. Mermaid code blocks and any diagram syntax must be preserved exactly as-is.

**2c. `notebook_describe(notebook_id)`** — returns an AI-generated notebook overview. Use this to identify the list of distinct APIs covered by the notebook. Each API name discovered here drives one output file in Step 7.

---

## Step 3 — Extract Documentation Content via notebook_query (run in parallel)

Run all five queries simultaneously against the notebook. These queries are targeted at populating the specific sections required by the `gen-docs-implementation.mdc` canonical structure.

If the notebook has 50 or more sources, use `notebook_query_start` for each query and poll `notebook_query_status` every 5 seconds until `status: completed`.

| Query ID | Question to send |
|---|---|
| Q-overview | "For each API in this notebook, list: technical name (e.g., drupal-exp-api), application business name, business domain, API type (Experience/Process/System), purpose in 1–2 sentences, healthcheck endpoints (GET /ping, GET /health-check), downstream Mule APIs it depends on, Insulet connector libraries used (e.g., Common Error Handler, Notification Connector, Common Logger), other libraries/connectors used (e.g., Salesforce Connector, HTTP Request Connector), API Manager policies applied (e.g., Client ID Enforcement, JWT Validation), and Parent-POM coordinates" |
| Q-nfr | "For each API, list the Non-Functional Requirements: authentication mechanism per endpoint, response time SLA (e.g., 95% within 60 seconds), expected transaction throughput (per minute or per day), spike control policy (Yes/No, and threshold if Yes), rate-limiting SLA (Yes/No, and limit if Yes)" |
| Q-endpoints | "For each API, list all endpoints with: HTTP method, resource path, brief description, all input fields (field name, data type, mandatory yes/no, validation rules or picklist values), and the success response schema (HTTP status code, response fields)" |
| Q-mapping | "For each API endpoint, describe the backend mapping and orchestration logic in detail: what downstream API calls are made and in what order, what fields from the input are mapped to what target fields (source field → target field → target object), what conditional logic applies (e.g., if leadSource == X then inject task), what Salesforce objects are written to" |
| Q-errors | "For each API, list: all HTTP status codes returned with their trigger conditions, the standard error response format, how errors are logged (e.g., async POST to Integration_Error__c), and any known edge cases or open issues" |

Collect the full response text for each query. Do not summarise or truncate the answers.

---

## Step 4 — Extract Source Summaries (run in parallel with Step 3)

For each `source_id` from Step 2a, call `source_describe(source_id)`. This returns keyword chips and a short AI-generated summary paragraph per source. Use these to cross-check the API names discovered in Step 2c and to enrich the §4 Interface Specifications sections.

Do **not** call `source_get_content` on any source — this is intentionally omitted to keep run time short.

---

## Step 5 — Resolve Confluence Links (optional)

Scan all text collected in Steps 3 and 4 for Confluence URLs matching either pattern:
```
https://insulet.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/{SPACE}/pages/{PAGE-ID}/{SLUG}
https://confluence.prod.insulet.com/wiki/spaces/{SPACE}/pages/{PAGE-ID}/{SLUG}
```

**If Confluence URLs are found:**
1. Extract the numeric `{PAGE-ID}` from each unique URL
2. Call `confluence_get_page(page_id, include_body=true)` via the Atlassian MCP (`user-atlassian-guardrails`)
3. Strip any HTML markup from the returned body text
4. Use the fetched content as enrichment context when writing §4 Backend Mapping & Logic sections — do not surface the raw page body in the output file

**If no Confluence URLs are found:** skip this step entirely and continue to Step 6. Do not block or report an error.

---

## Step 6 — Identify APIs and Derive Output File Names

From the responses collected in Steps 2c, 3 (Q-overview), and 4:

1. Build a deduplicated list of distinct API technical names (e.g., `drupal-exp-api`, `leads-prc-api`, `sfl-crm-persona-sys-api`)
2. For each API, derive the output filename:
   ```
   filename = {api-technical-name}.md
   e.g. drupal-exp-api.md, leads-prc-api.md
   ```
3. Output location: `agents/design/examples/{filename}`

---

## Step 7 — Write One Output File Per API

For each API identified in Step 6, write a Markdown file to `agents/design/examples/{api-technical-name}.md`. Overwrite any existing file with the same name.

Each file must follow the exact canonical section structure below. Populate every field from the query responses collected in Steps 3–5. For any field that cannot be determined from notebook content alone (i.e., requires actual code, deployment config, or Exchange metadata), insert `TBC` as the value — do **not** omit the row or invent a value.

---

